{"name":"Crack Willow","hex":"#b0a470","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crack willow","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A of willow native to Europe and western Asia (Salix fragilis)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crack_willow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salix × fragilis","blocks":[{"text":"Salix × fragilis, with the common names crack willow and brittle willow, is a hybrid species of willow native to Europe and Western Asia. It is native to riparian habitats, usually found growing beside rivers and streams, and in marshes and water meadow channels. It is a hybrid between Salix euxina and Salix alba, and is very variable, with forms linking both parents."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Salix_fragilis_collage.jpg/330px-Salix_fragilis_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salix_%C3%97_fragilis"},{"title":"Willow","blocks":[{"text":"Willows, also called sallows and osiers, of the genus Salix, comprise around 350 species of typically deciduous trees and shrubs. They are primarily found on moist soils in cold and temperate regions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Salix_alba_Morton.jpg/330px-Salix_alba_Morton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Salix_fragilis_002.jpg/500px-Salix_fragilis_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Salix_fragilis_002.jpg/1280px-Salix_fragilis_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Salix_fragilis_002.jpg/500px-Salix_fragilis_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&u